et; branchPending=false } else { nPC = nPC+4 }`.\n   \n   Wait that's not quite right either. Let me think again.\n\n   The PC/nPC method (from SPIM/computer architecture textbooks):\n   - Initialize: PC = entry, nPC = entry + 4\n   - Each cycle:\n     - instr = mem[PC]\n     - execute(instr): for non-branch, do nothing special. For taken branch with target T: set `nPC = T` (this becomes the new nPC AFTER the delay slot). But we also need... \n\n   Hmm, the issue: when we execute the branch at PC, the delay slot is at PC+4 = nPC. After executing the branch, we set PC=nPC (the delay slot) and we want nPC=T (the target). So:\n   - branch taken: temporarily, we want the NEXT instruction (delay slot) at nPC, and the one after that at T.\n   - So set: `PC = nPC` (delay slot), `nPC = T`. \n   - But the default end-of-cycle is `PC = nPC; nPC = PC + 4` (i.e., old nPC). \n\n   The textbook solution uses a separate mechanism. Let me use this concrete approach which I know works:\n\n   ```\n   let pc = ENTRY;\n   while (true) {\n     const instr = read32(pc);\n     const nextSeq = pc + 4;\n     let nextPc = nextSeq;       // default\n     // execute; branches set `nextPc` to target AND set a flag to skip one\n     ...\n   }\n   ```\n   \n   This doesn't naturally handle delay slots. The robust approach used by many JS MIPS emulators:\n\n   ```\n   let pc = ENTRY;\n   let npc = ENTRY + 4;\n   while (true) {\n     const instr = read32(pc);\n     let advancePc = npc;        // where to go after this instr\n     let advanceNpc = npc + 4;   // default next-next\n     switch (...) {\n       case BRANCH:\n         if (cond) {\n           advancePc = npc;          // delay slot first\n           advanceNpc = target;      // then branch target\n         }\n         // if not taken, advancePc=npc, advanceNpc=npc+4 (default)\n         break;\n       case JUMP (j/jal):\n         advancePc = npc;            // delay slot\n         advanceNpc = target;\n         break;\n       case JR/JALR: