cepts Uint8Array. Good.\n\n  Return value = bytes read (>=0) or throw on error. On EOF, returns 0. On error, I catch and return -errno.\n\n  Actually, for performance, reading the WAD: Doom reads the WAD in chunks. fread -> read syscall. The WAD is 4MB. Doom reads it in pieces. Each read syscall does fs.readSync. That's fine.\n\nFor write: a0=fd, a1=buf(addr), a2=count. `fs.writeSync(fd, mem8, off, count)` (position null = current). Returns bytes written. For fd 1 (stdout) or 2 (stderr), fs.writeSync(1, ...) writes to stdout. That works! Actually, fd 1 and 2 are real OS file descriptors (stdout/stderr). fs.writeSync(1, buffer, offset, length) writes to stdout. \n\n  Wait, but I'm allocating my own fd table starting at 3. fds 0,1,2 are the OS standard ones. I can use fs.writeSync/readSync with the real fd numbers directly for 0,1,2. For fd>=3, I use the real fd from fs.openSync. So actually I can just use the real fd numbers throughout! openSync returns a real fd (>=3 typically), and I return that as the MIPS fd. Then read/write/close use that real fd. No mapping needed! fds 0,1,2 are stdin/stdout/stderr (real). \n\n  This is the simplest: pass through real fds. The only risk: if the MIPS program assumes specific fd numbers. But Doom just uses whatever open returns. So pass-through is fine.\n\n  So:\n  - open: fd = fs.openSync(path, flags, mode); return fd.\n  - read: n = fs.readSync(fd, mem8, off, count, null); return n.\n  - write: n = fs.writeSync(fd, mem8, off, count, null); return n.  (for fd 1/2, writes to stdout/stderr)\n  - close: fs.closeSync(fd); return 0.\n  - lseek: pos = fs... hmm, lseek needs to set file position. Node doesn't have lseekSync directly. But I can use fs.readSync/writeSync with position parameter, OR track position manually. Actually, for lseek, I need to get/set the file offset. \n\n    Node doesn't expose lseek directly. Options:\n    1. Track file positions in a Map<fd, position>. For read/write, use the tracked position (pass as positi