'll keep a variable `frameFd = -1`. On open, if path === \"/tmp/frame.bmp\", set frameFd = returned fd. On close, if fd === frameFd, set haltAfterFrame = true (and frameFd = -1). After executing, if haltAfterFrame, stop.\n\nWait, but I want to stop AFTER the close completes (frame is saved). So in the close handler, after fs.closeSync, if fd was frameFd, set a halt flag. Then the main loop checks the halt flag and breaks.\n\nLet me now also handle: the write to /tmp/frame.bmp must produce a valid BMP. The binary constructs the BMP in MIPS memory and writes it via fwrite (write syscall). I just write mem8 bytes to the file. The BMP format is produced by the binary. Good.\n\nNow, let me also double-check the BMP: writeBMPFile writes `file_size = 54 + image_size` bytes where image_size = 640*400*4. But the BMP header in the code is a bit unusual (it allocates bmp+2, writes signature at bmp, header at bmp+2, etc.). The fwrite writes `bmp` for `file_size` bytes. The BMP is 54 + 640*400*4 = 54 + 1024000 = 1024054 bytes. It's a 32-bit BGRA BMP. The grader will check it's a valid BMP with the title screen. As long as I faithfully write the bytes, it's correct.\n\nNow, let me write the vm.js. This will be a large file. Let me write it carefully.\n\nLet me reconsider the memory size and layout once more:\n- BASE = 0x00400000\n- Segments:\n  - seg0: vaddr 0x400000, filesz 0x56514, memsz 0x56514 (text+rodata, r-x)\n  - seg1: vaddr 0x466520, filesz 0xeb80, memsz 0x4004b270 (data+bss, rw)\n- BSS top = 0x466520 + 0x4004b270 = 0x404b1790\n- I need memory from 0x400000 to at least 0x404b1790, plus stack.\n- Let me allocate MEM_SIZE to cover up to 0x40600000 (with stack at top). MEM_SIZE = 0x40600000 - 0x400000 = 0x40200000 = 1,075,838,976 bytes (~1.003 GB). \n\n  Wait, 0x40200000 = 1075838976? 0x40000000 = 1073741824. 0x40200000 = 1073741824 + 2097152 = 1075838976. Yes ~1.003GB. Covers 0x400000 to 0x40600000. BSS top 0x404b1790 < 0x40600000. Stack top at 0x405fff00. Stack space = 0x